mockIdentityApi not returning overridden values as expected

### 🔎 Search Terms

```plain
renderInTestApp, mockIdenitytyApi, "override mock identity", "unable to override mock identity"
```

### 🗃️ Project Area

Unknown

### 📝 Description & Context

## Background

I'm upgrading some old tests to use the new front end testing utilities. Here's an excerpt of the old code that I'm replacing:

```js
import { renderInTestApp, TestApiProvider } from from '@backstage/test-utils';

...

renderInTestApp(
  <TestApiProvider apis={[
    [configApiRef, mockConfig],
    [identityApiRef, mockIdentityApi],
  ]}>
    <MyComponent entity={entity} />
  </TestApiProvider>
);
```

I've replaced this with the updated method of mocking these APIs ([based on the docs](https://[ref]#with-renderintestapp)):

```js
import { mockApis, renderInTestApp } from from '@backstage/frontend-test-utils';

...

renderInTestApp(<MyComponent entity={entity} />, {
  apis: [
    // look at this custom user
    mockApis.identity({ userEntityRef: 'user:default/i-just-made-this-up' }),
  ],
  config: { backend: { baseUrl } },
});
```

The assertion for this test remains unchanged:

```js
expect(customMock).toHaveBeencalledWith(
  entity,
  'i-just-made-this-up',  // <-- what I passed in above
  baseUrl,
  unrelatedValue,
);
```

## Actual Behavior

This test fails now after updating to the new `frontend-test-utils`, because the function is instead called with a user value that I cannot locate. The actual call to this function is as follows:

```js
customMock(
  entity,
  'guest',  // <-- Where'd this come from?
  baseUrl,
  unrelatedValue,
);
```

### 👍 Expected Behavior

My expectation is that the `mockIdentityApi` consumes and uses the `userEntityRef` that I passed in. There's a [short blurb in the documentation](https://[ref]#available-mock-apis) to support my expectation:

> mockApis.identity(options?) -- Configurable user ref, ownership, token, profile

I did some debugging and editing of the `@backstage/frontend-test-utils` in the `node_modules` directory and I have confirmed that the expected code is being called and my custom `userEntityRef` is making it into the code. I'm just not seeing exactly where the failure is though.

## Other Oddities

Also something interesting is `user:default/guest` only appears in the `CHANGELOG.md` of the `frontend-test-utils` plugin. The code defaults to `user:default/test`:

```shell
➜  custom-form git:(main) ✗ ag --js 'default/' node_modules/@backstage/frontend-test-utils/dist
node_modules/@backstage/frontend-test-utils/dist/apis/mockApis.esm.js
114:      userEntityRef = "user:default/test",
115:      ownershipEntityRefs = ["user:default/test"],
➜  custom-form git:(main) ✗ ag --js 'default/guest' node_modules/@backstage/frontend-test-utils/dist
➜  custom-form git:(main) ✗
```

So that `default/test` user is being overridden by `default/guest` user by some other part of the code. I was planning to submit the fix, but I was unable to determine the source of this mystery value.

### 🥾 Reproduction steps

Here's a test file that confirms my findings (`meh.test.tsx`):

```js
import { ErrorBoundary } from '@backstage/core-components';
import {
  identityApiRef,
  useApi,
} from '@backstage/core-plugin-api';
import {
  mockApis,
  renderInTestApp,
} from '@backstage/frontend-test-utils';

import { screen, waitFor } from '@testing-library/react';
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

const useData = () => {
  const identityApi = useApi(identityApiRef);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [value, setValue] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;

    identityApi.getBackstageIdentity()
      .then((output: any) => {
        console.log({ignore, loading, output});
        if (ignore) {
          return;
        }
        setValue(output.userEntityRef);
      })
      .catch((error: any) => {
        setError(error);
      })
      .finally(() => {
        if (ignore) {
          return;
        }
        setLoading(false);
      })
    ;

    return () => {
      ignore = true;
    };
  });

  return {
    error,
    loading,
    value,
  };
};

const Catpants = () => {
  const state = useData();

  return (
    <ErrorBoundary>
     {state.loading
        ? <div>Loading...</div>
        : state.error
          ? <div>Error: {state.error.message}</div>
          : <h1>{state.value}</h1>
      }
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );

};

describe('identity api', () => {
  it('should render with custom user entity', async () => {
    renderInTestApp(<Catpants />, {
      apis: [
        mockApis.identity({ userEntityRef: 'user:default/i-just-made-this-up' }),
      ],
    });
    await waitFor(() => {
      expect(screen.queryByText('Loading...')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
    });
    expect(screen.getByText('user:default/i-just-made-this-up')).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it('should render with test user entity', async () => {
    renderInTestApp(<Catpants />, {
      apis: [mockApis.identity()],
    });

    await waitFor(() => {
      expect(screen.queryByText('Loading...')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
    });
    expect(screen.getByText('user:default/test')).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it('should not render guest user entity', async () => {
    renderInTestApp(<Catpants />, {
      apis: [
        mockApis.identity({ userEntityRef: 'user:default/i-just-made-this-up' }),
      ],
    });
    await waitFor(() => {
      expect(screen.queryByText('Loading...')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
    });
    expect(screen.getByText('user:default/guest')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
  });
});

```

It contains 3 individual test that I would expect to pass.

1. My expected input should be in the output.
2. Without a custom value, `user:default/test` should be the value.
3. `user:default/guest` should not be in the output. (Sort of a duplicate of 1 here)

