Store TechDocs source Markdown alongside generated HTML

### 🔎 Search Terms

```plain
techdocs, source markdown, MCP AI, _sources, preserve, generator
```

### 🗃️ Project Area

TechDocs

### 🔖 Need

TechDocs discards the original Markdown source files after generating HTML with MkDocs. Only the rendered HTML, static assets, and `techdocs_metadata.json` are published to storage. Any consumer that needs the original content has to either reverse-engineer the HTML or fetch the source from the originating repository separately.

### AI and MCP tool access

Backstage adopters are exposing documentation to AI assistants via MCP. Issue [ref] and PR #33412 show community demand for this. MkDocs Material HTML pages contain navigation sidebars, headers, footers, search chrome, and deeply nested wrapper elements, all of which are noise for AI consumers. Benchmarks show Markdown uses 60-90% fewer tokens than equivalent HTML and produces 23-40% higher comprehension accuracy across major LLMs (see References below).

Without stored sources, MCP tools have to either convert HTML back to Markdown at request time (losing information for MkDocs-specific elements like admonitions and PlantUML diagrams compiled to images at build time) or fetch from the source repository on every request (adding latency and requiring SCM authentication).

### Generator coupling

TechDocs is tightly coupled to MkDocs. Any HTML-to-Markdown conversion needs MkDocs-specific custom rules for admonitions (`div.admonition`), tabbed content (`div.tabbed-set`), collapsible sections (`<details>`), and Pygments-highlighted code blocks. If an adopter swaps MkDocs for another generator, the HTML structure changes completely and the converter breaks.

Stored source Markdown is generator-agnostic. It's the same regardless of which tool builds it.

### Search quality

The existing TechDocs search collator indexes content via `search/search_index.json`, which strips all formatting. Headings, code blocks, links, and tables are flattened to plain text. Issue [ref] rejected this for AI consumption. Stored source Markdown preserves document structure, which would allow section-level search results, code block extraction, and better chunking for RAG pipelines.

### 📝 Proposal

After the generator build completes and before `createOrUpdateMetadata()` runs, copy the prepared source tree into a `_sources/` subdirectory of the output directory. The publisher uploads it as part of its normal `publish()` call. The backend serves source files through the existing static docs route.

### Storage layout

```
{namespace}/{kind}/{name}/
  index.html                     # existing generated HTML
  sub-page/index.html            # existing generated HTML
  assets/...                     # existing static assets
  search/search_index.json       # existing search index
  techdocs_metadata.json         # existing metadata
  _sources/                      # new — prepared source tree
    mkdocs.yml                   # generator config (MkDocs example)
    docs/                        # source Markdown files
      index.md
      sub-page.md
      assets/
        diagram.png
```

The `_sources/` directory contains only the documentation content — the generator config file and the docs directory — not the full prepared source tree. For MkDocs this means `mkdocs.yml` and the `docs/` directory (or whatever `docs_dir` is configured to). Files outside the docs directory (source code, CI configs, other repo content) are never copied.

### Implementation

TechDocs can be built in two ways: locally inside a Backstage instance (via `DocsBuilder`) or externally using the TechDocs CLI (`techdocs-cli generate`). The generator must support both paths.

The core copy logic lives in `TechdocsGenerator.run()` in `plugins/techdocs-node/src/stages/generate/techdocs.ts`. The generator receives a `preserveSources` boolean on `GeneratorRunOptions`. The caller resolves it:

- **`DocsBuilder`** (Backstage instance) — resolves from entity annotation + `app-config.yaml` global setting
- **`techdocs-cli generate`** — resolves from a new `--include-sources` CLI flag

The generator doesn't need to know about entities or annotations. It just gets told whether to include sources.

The copy only includes the documentation content (the generator config file and the docs directory), not the entire prepared source tree. When an entity uses `backstage.io/techdocs-ref: dir:.`, the `inputDir` is the full repository root, which may contain source code, CI configs, or sensitive files. Copying all of that into publicly-served storage would be a security risk.

The `docsDir` is already resolved from `mkdocs.yml` via `validateMkdocsYaml()` earlier in the generate step, so it's available at this point:

```typescript
// In GeneratorRunOptions (types.ts)
export type GeneratorRunOptions = {
  // ... existing fields ...
  preserveSources?: boolean;
  sourceExcludes?: string[];
};

// In TechdocsGenerator — a private method called from run()
private async copySources(options: {
  inputDir: string;
  outputDir: string;
  docsDir: string | undefined;
  sourceExcludes?: string[];
  logger: LoggerService;
}): Promise<void> {
  const { inputDir, outputDir, docsDir, sourceExcludes, logger } = options;
  const sourcesDir = path.join(outputDir, '_sources');
  await fs.ensureDir(sourcesDir);

  const filter = createSourceExcludeFilter(sourceExcludes);

  const configFile = path.join(inputDir, 'mkdocs.yml');
  if (await fs.pathExists(configFile)) {
    await fs.copy(configFile, path.join(sourcesDir, 'mkdocs.yml'));
  }

  const resolvedDocsDir = docsDir ?? 'docs';
  const docsSource = path.join(inputDir, resolvedDocsDir);
  if (await fs.pathExists(docsSource)) {
    await fs.copy(docsSource, path.join(sourcesDir, resolvedDocsDir), {
      filter,
    });
  }

  logger.info(`Preserved source files in ${sourcesDir}`);
}

// Called from run() after build, before metadata creation:
if (options.preserveSources) {
  await this.copySources({
    inputDir, outputDir, docsDir,
    sourceExcludes: options.sourceExcludes,
    logger: childLogger,
  });
}
```

The exclude filter is an exported `@internal` function for testability:

```typescript
function createSourceExcludeFilter(sourceExcludes?: string[]): (filePath: string) => boolean {
  const allExcludes = [...BUILT_IN_EXCLUDES, ...(sourceExcludes ?? [])];
  return (filePath: string): boolean => {
    const basename = path.basename(filePath);
    return !allExcludes.some(pattern => {
      if (pattern.startsWith('*.')) {
        return basename.endsWith(pattern.slice(1));
      }
      return basename === pattern;
    });
  };
}
```

#### Backstage instance path (`DocsBuilder`)

`DocsBuilder` in `plugins/techdocs-backend/src/DocsBuilder/builder.ts` has a private method that resolves the annotation, falling back to the global config:

```typescript
private resolvePreserveSources(): boolean {
  const annotation =
    this.entity.metadata.annotations?.['backstage.io/techdocs-source-storage'];
  if (annotation === 'enabled') return true;
  if (annotation === 'disabled') return false;
  return (
    this.config.getOptionalBoolean('techdocs.generator.preserveSources.enabled') ?? false
  );
}

// In DocsBuilder.build():
await this.generator.run({
  // ... existing options ...
  preserveSources: this.resolvePreserveSources(),
  sourceExcludes: this.config.getOptionalStringArray(
    'techdocs.generator.preserveSources.excludes',
  ),
});
```

#### CLI path (`techdocs-cli generate`)

The CLI has no entity and no `app-config.yaml`, so the annotation and global config do not apply. Instead, the CLI adds `--include-sources` and `--source-excludes` flags:

```typescript
// In commands/index.ts — added to the generate command:
.option(
  '--include-sources',
  'Include source Markdown files in the generated output under _sources/.',
  false,
)
.option(
  '--source-excludes <PATTERNS...>',
  'Glob patterns to exclude from _sources/ (for example, "*.png" "*.jpg").',
  [],
)

// In commands/generate/generate.ts:
await techdocsGenerator.run({
  // ... existing options ...
  preserveSources: opts.includeSources,
  sourceExcludes: opts.sourceExcludes,
});
```

#### How the three mechanisms interact

| Mechanism | Applies to | Controls |
|-----------|-----------|----------|
| `app-config.yaml` `preserveSources.enabled` | Backstage instance builds | Global default (on/off) |
| `app-config.yaml` `preserveSources.excludes` | Backstage instance builds | Global exclude patterns |
| `backstage.io/techdocs-source-storage` annotation | Backstage instance builds | Per-entity override of the global default |
| `--include-sources` CLI flag | CLI builds | Opt-in (replaces global config + annotation) |
| `--source-excludes` CLI flag | CLI builds | Exclude patterns (replaces global config excludes) |

The annotation only overrides **whether** sources are stored, not **what** is excluded. Excludes are always resolved at the instance level (from `app-config.yaml`) or CLI level (from `--source-excludes`). Built-in excludes (`.git/`, `node_modules/`, etc.) apply in both paths.

This does make the copy logic aware of MkDocs conventions (`mkdocs.yml`, `docs_dir`). Alternative generators would need to specify their own config file name and content directory. The `GeneratorBase` interface could be extended with a method that returns the source paths to preserve, making this extensible without hardcoding generator-specific knowledge in the copy logic.

The copy must happen **after** the build and **before** `createOrUpdateMetadata()`:

1. **After build**: the generator (e.g., `mkdocs build -d <outputDir>`) takes ownership of the output directory and may clean it before writing. Copying before the build risks the generator wiping the source files.
2. **Before metadata**: `createOrUpdateMetadata()` calls `getFileTreeRecursively()` on the output directory to populate the `files` array in `techdocs_metadata.json`. The `_sources/` files must be present so they are tracked for stale file cleanup during republishing.

### Configuration

Source storage has two layers of control: a global instance-level configuration and a per-entity annotation opt-in.

#### Global configuration (instance-level)

The global config controls the default behavior and exclude patterns for the entire Backstage instance:

```yaml
techdocs:
  generator:
    preserveSources:
      enabled: true  # default: false — global default for all entities
      excludes:      # optional — glob patterns to exclude from _sources/
        - '*.png'
        - '*.jpg'
        - '*.gif'
        - '*.svg'
```

A set of built-in excludes is always applied regardless of configuration:

- `.git/`
- `node_modules/`
- `__pycache__/`
- `.venv/`
- `*.pyc`

Adopter-provided `excludes` extend the built-in list. They don't replace it.

#### Per-entity opt-in (annotation)

Individual entities can opt in using the `backstage.io/techdocs-source-storage` annotation:

```yaml
apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1
kind: Component
metadata:
  name: my-service
  annotations:
    backstage.io/techdocs-ref: dir:.
    backstage.io/techdocs-source-storage: enabled
```

The annotation accepts two values:
- `enabled` — store sources for this entity (regardless of the global default)
- `disabled` — do not store sources for this entity (regardless of the global default)

When the annotation is absent, the entity falls back to the global `preserveSources.enabled` setting.

This gives adopters two deployment patterns:

1. **Opt-in per entity** (global `enabled: false`, annotation `enabled` on specific entities) — for gradual rollout or when only some docs need AI/MCP access.
2. **Global on, opt-out per entity** (global `enabled: true`, annotation `disabled` on specific entities) — for broad adoption with exceptions for entities with large source trees or sensitive content.

Exclude patterns are always global. Per-entity excludes would add complexity without much benefit since the patterns are about file types (images, compiled assets), not content.

### What doesn't change

`PublishRequest`, `PublisherBase`, and all five publisher implementations are untouched. Publishers already upload everything in the output directory. Source files are served through the existing `GET /static/docs/{namespace}/{kind}/{name}/*` route, so no new API routes are needed. The frontend continues to fetch and render HTML. Stale file cleanup also works without changes because `_sources/` files are included in `techdocs_metadata.json`'s `files` array, so cloud publishers correctly remove old source files on republish.

### Security considerations

- The copy only includes the generator config file and the `docs_dir`. When `backstage.io/techdocs-ref: dir:.` points to a repo root, the prepared `inputDir` contains the entire repository, including source code, CI configs, and potentially sensitive files. Only the documentation content directory is copied into `_sources/`.
- Source files within the docs directory may include content excluded from the generated output (for example, Markdown files not listed in the `nav` section, or draft pages excluded by plugins). Adopters should be aware that enabling source storage makes all docs directory content accessible, not just the pages that appear in the generated site.
- The `mkdocs.yml` is the sanitized version — the copy happens after `sanitizeMkdocsYml()` has removed unsupported keys.
- Path traversal protection already exists in the prepare step (`validateDocsDirectory()`).

### Storage impact

Source Markdown is typically much smaller than generated HTML (no theme assets, no duplicated navigation, no search index). Expect roughly 10–30% increase in storage footprint.

### 1. HTML-to-Markdown conversion at request time

Convert stored HTML to Markdown when an AI tool requests content, using Turndown or node-html-markdown. This is the approach in PR #33412.

Works without pipeline changes but has drawbacks: information loss for MkDocs-specific elements (admonitions, tabbed content, PlantUML compiled to images), generator-specific conversion rules needed, MkDocs Material boilerplate must be stripped before converting, and every request pays the conversion cost. It also adds a new dependency (Turndown or node-html-markdown) and a set of custom conversion rules to the TechDocs plugin surface that must be maintained and updated whenever the generator's HTML output changes — more code to own in an already complex plugin.

Still valuable as a fallback for deployments that haven't enabled source storage, but not a substitute.

### 2. Fetch from source repository on demand

Use the entity's `backstage.io/techdocs-ref` annotation and `UrlReaderService` to fetch Markdown from the repo.

Requires SCM authentication that may not be available, risks staleness (repo may have changed since last build), adds network latency, and misses content injected by build-time plugins (snippets, includes).

This approach also consumes SCM API rate limits on every content request. AI tools can be chatty. A single conversation might fetch dozens of documentation pages, and each fetch is an API call against GitHub, GitLab, or whatever SCM hosts the source. At scale, this creates rate limit pressure on the same SCM APIs that the catalog, scaffolder, and other plugins already depend on.

Storing sources in cloud storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob) avoids this. The source files are copied once during the TechDocs build, which already fetches from the SCM as part of the prepare step, and served from storage afterward. No additional SCM API calls, no rate limit contention with other Backstage subsystems.

### 3. Use `search_index.json`

MkDocs generates `search/search_index.json` with pre-extracted text per page.

All formatting is stripped — no headings, code blocks, links, or tables. Explicitly rejected in [ref]. Suitable for keyword search only.

### 4. Separate storage path (`sources/{namespace}/{kind}/{name}/`)

Store sources in a parallel path instead of a `_sources/` subdirectory.

Requires publisher changes, new routes, and new cleanup handling. No clear benefit over the subdirectory approach which reuses all existing infrastructure.

